@startuml interrupt_context
!theme plain
title Interrupt Context: IRQ → Tasklet → Workqueue

participant Hardware
participant IRQ as "IRQ Handler\n(hardirq context)"
participant Tasklet as "Tasklet\n(soft-irq context)"
participant Workqueue as "Workqueue\n(kthread context)"
participant Driver as "Driver Code"

== Hardware Interrupt ==
Hardware -> IRQ: Interrupt signal
activate IRQ
note right of IRQ
  Cannot sleep
  Cannot use mutex
  No memory alloc (GFP_KERNEL)
  Minimize latency
end note

IRQ -> IRQ: Read status register
IRQ -> IRQ: ACK interrupt
IRQ -> Driver: Check if handled

alt Not handled
  IRQ --> Hardware: IRQ_NONE
  deactivate IRQ
else Handled

  == Schedule Deferred Work ==
  alt Fast processing possible
    IRQ -> IRQ: Do work now
    IRQ --> Hardware: IRQ_HANDLED
  else Slow work needed
    IRQ -> Tasklet: tasklet_schedule()
    IRQ --> Hardware: IRQ_HANDLED
  end

  deactivate IRQ
end

== Tasklet (soft-irq) ==
activate Tasklet
note right of Tasklet
  Runs soon after IRQ returns
  Lower priority than hardirq
  Cannot sleep (traditional)
  Can use spinlocks
  Can work with atomic allocations
end note

Tasklet -> Driver: Process deferred work
Tasklet -> Driver: For longer operations,\nqueue_work()

alt Work complete
  Tasklet --> Hardware: Done
  deactivate Tasklet
else Need to sleep
  Tasklet -> Workqueue: queue_work()
  Tasklet --> Hardware: Done
  deactivate Tasklet
end

== Workqueue (kthread) ==
activate Workqueue
note right of Workqueue
  Runs in kernel thread context
  Can sleep (wait for locks)
  Can use mutex
  Can allocate with GFP_KERNEL
  Slower but more flexible
end note

Workqueue -> Driver: Do slow work
Driver -> Driver: File I/O
Driver -> Driver: Wait for devices
Workqueue --> Hardware: Work complete
deactivate Workqueue

note right of Hardware
  Interrupt latency breakdown:
  - Hardirq: 1-10 µs (minimal)
  - Tasklet: 10-100 µs (process packets)
  - Workqueue: 100+ µs (flexible)
end note

@enduml
